# Hemma settings — copy to .env (read from the folder Hemma is started in, or pass
# --env FILE) and fill in. Real environment variables (e.g. systemd's Environment=) win
# over this file; command line flags win over both.
# On a Pi set up with deploy/install-pi.sh this file is /etc/hemma/hemma.env
# (root-owned; the service can read it but not change it).

# --- sign-in -------------------------------------------------------------
# Secret server-wide salt mixed into every password hash. Hemma makes one itself on
# its first start and writes it into this file (readable by its owner only); to make
# one by hand: ./hemma --gen-salt
# Keep it out of git and backups-of-the-database: a copied hemma.db is useless
# without it. Changing it later invalidates every password.
HEMMA_AUTH_SALT=

# The one-time setup code the welcome page asks for, so that only someone who can see
# this machine (not anyone on the Wi-Fi who gets there first) creates the admin. Leave
# it out and Hemma makes one and prints it in its window/log at start; the Raspberry Pi
# installer writes one here and shows it. Useless once the admin exists. Make one by
# hand: ./hemma --gen-setup-code
# HEMMA_SETUP_CODE=

# The first admin. Normally you leave these out: the first visit to Hemma shows a
# welcome page where you create the admin in the browser (and get recovery codes).
# For a headless install, set them instead: the admin is created on the first start
# while the household has no admin yet, and the welcome page never appears.
# (If a person with this name already exists without a sign-in, they become the admin.)
# The password is used only that once: remove it after you've signed in. From then on
# people are managed in Hemma (Household) - deleting or renaming this admin there is
# final; it does not come back on the next start. See docs/accounts.md.
# HEMMA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# HEMMA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# HEMMA_ADMIN_NAME="Your Name"
# Forgot a password and the recovery codes? On the server, run (same --env and --data
# as the service, as the user it runs as):  hemma --reset-password USERNAME
# No admin account left at all? Set HEMMA_ADMIN_RESET=1 together with HEMMA_ADMIN_USER
# and a NEW HEMMA_ADMIN_PASSWORD and restart: that user becomes an admin with this
# password (re-created if it was deleted) and is signed out everywhere. It is applied once
# per password, so a forgotten line does no harm - but remove the lines afterwards.
# HEMMA_ADMIN_RESET=1

# Password hashing work factor (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 iterations). Higher = slower
# to guess, slower to sign in.
# HEMMA_KDF_ITERATIONS=120000

# --- server --------------------------------------------------------------
# HEMMA_HOST=0.0.0.0
# HEMMA_PORT=8080
# HEMMA_DATA=/var/lib/hemma
# Extra names Hemma answers to, besides IP addresses, single names ("hemma") and
# home-network names (hemma.local, *.lan, *.home.arpa, ...). ".suffix" = any name under it.
# HEMMA_HOSTS=hemma.example.com, .ts.net
